description:
A Promise in TypeScript is just like . . . a promise. Its just a commitment. A Promise has two possible outcomes:
- It's fulfilled: What it commited to do was done
- It's rejected: It failed to do what was commited.
The Promise Object represents the fulfillment or rejection of a promise, and you can run code while the promise is being fulfilled/rejected. Promises are the main way you write asynchronous code in TypeScript.
```ts
const promise = new Promise<string> /* The type in the < > is the type that is resolved */ ((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
        if (getRandomBool()) {
            resolve("resolved :)");
        } else {
            reject("rejected :(");
        }
    }, 1000);});
function getRandomBool(): boolean {
    return Math.random() < 0.5;
}
```
In the above code, the promise will be resolved :) if the random number is true and rejected :( otherwise. In the `new Promise` constructor, resolve and reject are functions provided by the runtime.
---
## Working with promises:
Promises have two main methods: [`.then`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/then), and [`.catch`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/catch). If it resolves, the `.then` method activates, and if it rejects, `.catch` activate. They both take a callback function.
---
## Assignment:
Create the updateVideo function, which should return a promise that after 500 milliseconds:
- if `currentStatus` is "In draft", resolve with `Succesfully updated video <videoName>`.
- if `currentStatus` is "Published" and `timeUploaded` is < 100, reject with `Video <videoName> hasn't been uploaded long enough to update.`
- otheriwse, resolve with `Published video <videoName> updated.`

